647
02:19:11,064 --> 02:19:14,863
As a teenager, he escaped Nazi Germany to the US,

648
02:19:15,123 --> 02:19:17,924
where his scientific intellect flourished after the war.

649
02:19:19,203 --> 02:19:21,984
Ralph Baer is one of the finest examples I can think of

650
02:19:21,984 --> 02:19:23,203
of inventive ingenuity.

651
02:19:24,043 --> 02:19:26,664
Ralph was the kind of person who was constantly thinking,

652
02:19:26,984 --> 02:19:30,824
you know, five to seven steps ahead of everybody else in society.

653
02:19:32,484 --> 02:19:35,664
During the late 1940s and 1950s,

654
02:19:36,043 --> 02:19:38,623
Ralph created everything from hi-fi accessories

655
02:19:38,623 --> 02:19:41,324
to snooping devices that observed the Soviet Union.

656
02:19:42,703 --> 02:19:45,724
He was also convinced television could be less passive

657
02:19:45,724 --> 02:19:46,884
and more interactive.

658
02:19:47,623 --> 02:19:51,263
It was a radical concept in the 1950s, so no one listened.

659
02:19:51,984 --> 02:19:55,404
At this point in time, it was absolutely insane

660
02:19:55,404 --> 02:19:58,363
to try to think of reacting to a television set

661
02:19:58,363 --> 02:20:00,144
in any way other than just viewing,

662
02:20:00,623 --> 02:20:02,424
in any way other than just being spoon-fed

663
02:20:02,424 --> 02:20:06,043
by whatever the networks or the small independent companies

664
02:20:06,043 --> 02:20:07,884
thought they could feed you.

665
02:20:08,463 --> 02:20:11,084
Basically, it was a medium where you just took it in.

666
02:20:11,203 --> 02:20:12,744
You had no say in what you saw,

667
02:20:13,064 --> 02:20:14,443
except for changing channels.

668
02:20:14,543 --> 02:20:17,424
And in those days, you didn't have that many channels to change.

669
02:20:17,424 --> 02:20:24,443
In 1966, Ralph was designing equipment for a military contractor.

670
02:20:25,144 --> 02:20:27,144
He was working with an alignment generator

671
02:20:27,144 --> 02:20:29,603
that created test lines on a TV screen.

672
02:20:30,123 --> 02:20:33,203
You had these testers that would put cross-hatches

673
02:20:33,203 --> 02:20:36,283
of horizontal lines and vertical lines on the screen

674
02:20:36,283 --> 02:20:38,084
so that you could test them and make sure they were working.

675
02:20:38,084 --> 02:20:44,004
The machine inspired him to imagine a completely new type of game.

676
02:20:45,804 --> 02:20:47,244
He realized this would be really fun

677
02:20:47,244 --> 02:20:48,943
if we could put it in the user's hands

678
02:20:48,943 --> 02:20:50,943
and the user could play with it.

679
02:20:51,824 --> 02:20:54,183
You know, what if I could bounce those lines around?

680
02:20:54,304 --> 02:20:56,963
What if I could chase a dot around on the screen?

681
02:20:56,963 --> 02:21:00,843
To turn his idea into reality,

682
02:21:01,363 --> 02:21:03,603
he needed to find a way to generate a signal

683
02:21:03,603 --> 02:21:05,543
that changed those static lines

684
02:21:05,543 --> 02:21:08,023
into symbols that moved around the screen.

685
02:21:08,023 --> 02:21:28,023
You know, what if I could do?

686
02:21:12,244 --> 02:21:18,244
It was in September 1966, while sitting at a bus stop, that it started to fall into place.

687
02:21:21,780 --> 02:21:27,780
Ralph pulls out his spiral bound notebook, he starts making notes, he creates this memo.

688
02:21:27,780 --> 02:21:35,780
In the first page he visualizes, you know, a whole gamut of game types that could be useful in video games.

689
02:21:36,780 --> 02:21:46,780
Ralph realized that if a radio frequency or RF signal could display moving pictures on a TV screen, it should also be able to generate game symbols.

690
02:21:49,940 --> 02:21:51,940
You getting on buddy?

691
02:21:54,708 --> 02:22:06,108
Ralph knew it would be difficult to persuade people at the office about his new idea, where the focus was on serious military technology. So he started to develop his TV game in secret.

692
02:22:06,108 --> 02:22:13,908
At this point in time, Ralph has enough people reporting to him and enough budget to be able to disguise what he's doing.

693
02:22:13,908 --> 02:22:18,708
Ralph set to work using the components of an alignment generator.

694
02:22:21,907 --> 02:22:33,907
He used a radio frequency oscillator to generate a video signal. Then a component called a sync generator synchronized that signal into one that the television understood.

695
02:22:33,907 --> 02:22:48,907
A modulator then sent the signal to the TV and placed it on channel 3 or 4. As the signal entered the TV, a spot appeared on the screen, which could move horizontally, vertically and change shape.

696
02:22:52,180 --> 02:22:57,180
He had proven that his concept worked. Ralph then built an actual game box.

697
02:22:59,180 --> 02:23:09,180
But it wasn't user friendly. He had used glass vacuum tubes to regulate the flow of electricity, and these took up too much space and blew.

698
02:23:12,595 --> 02:23:21,595
You couldn't put out a consumer game unit that was based on vacuum tubes because, you know, they would blow out and you would have to replace the tubes.

699
02:23:23,595 --> 02:23:31,595
However, new technology enabled Ralph to progress. He enlisted a talented colleague, Bill Harrison, who knew all about transistors.

700
02:23:32,595 --> 02:23:40,595
Tiny silicone semiconductors that performed the same function as vacuum tubes, but without the bulk or fragility.

701
02:23:43,572 --> 02:23:47,971
They also meant Ralph's game box could be smaller and more user-friendly.

702
02:23:51,172 --> 02:23:54,371
Finally, a prototype game box was completed.

703
02:23:54,971 --> 02:24:00,572
Each game came with a plastic overlay, such as a ping pong table, to make the game more realistic.

